    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2669565">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2669565">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2669565)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2669565">
																		Random Article Review #6, 12 October 2016
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973905" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1476305276 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Oct 2016 20:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2669565">
									

<p>The subject matter and the interpretation of the Foundation here are so radically different than what I'm interested in reading about that there's almost no way I can roll with it and support the piece. That's not something I would characterize as a fault with the author's work, however. There's a difference between &quot;This piece of writing is flawed&quot; and &quot;I don't like this piece of writing.&quot; All of the critique we do aside, the rating module is ultimately a question of yea or nay, and in this case my reaction to the piece demands nay.</p>
<p>That said, there are two issues here that I think tend to come up from time to time in other pieces on the wiki, to the detriment of overall quality:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Everyone is a nineteen-year-old on the internet.</strong> There's a tendency to write every character, whether they're an otherworldly alien being, a hardened field agent or a hapless civilian, like they're a teenager/twentysomething that the author has just talked to in #site19. The source of this problem is obvious, but a serious writer should be able to alter their own perspective when they're trying to present a character. &quot;Write what you know&quot; is the response that I always get to this criticism, but I think the attitude should be more along the lines of &quot;expand what you know.&quot; Things like the guy in this tale raging about his waifu and the two older men quoting scenes from Dr. Strangelove gave off this vibe to me, and it took me out of the narrative.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><strong>Super-aggressive whimsy.</strong> This tale features a lot of very detailed, very imaginative description throughout. When used well, this style of writing can be really evocative and can do a good job of establishing suspension of disbelief and getting a reader to buy what you're selling. I've noticed, however, that there are cases where this is done in such detail, with so many different things going on, and so many zany elements, that what I experience instead as a reader is sensory overload, and I feel like instead of reading a story, I'm being bombarded with stimuli. I have nothing against this kind of writing, and it's been done well in the past by this author, as well as in a few <a href="/a-simple-solution">recent</a> <a href="/melting-in-slow-motion">tales</a>. It's tricky, and I don't pretend to have any specific guidance where the border between &quot;gonzo, mindblowing piece of good writing&quot; and &quot;lolrandom grab bag of crazy stuff on the walls&quot; lay. But there's a tendency sometimes for people to just open the throttle on this kind of thing.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
